```java
@Mock OrderRepository mockRepo;
@Mock EmailService mockEmail;
OrderService orderService; // no @InjectMocks

@BeforeEach
void setUp() {
    orderService = new OrderService(mockRepo, mockEmail);
}
```
This is completely explicit — you see exactly what's being injected. Fails immediately at compile time if constructor signature changes. No magic.

@Mock is a pure Mockito annotation. It creates a mock object in memory and is processed by MockitoExtension. It has nothing to do with Spring — it just gives you a mock that you then inject into your class under test manually or via @InjectMocks. Tests using @Mock do not start a Spring context and run very fast.

@MockBean is a Spring Boot annotation that registers a Mockito mock as a bean in the Spring application context. When you write @MockBean private UserRepository userRepository in a @SpringBootTest test, Spring Boot replaces the real UserRepository bean in the context with a mock. Any other Spring bean that depends on UserRepository will get the mock injected automatically. This is useful in integration tests where you want most of the real application wired up but need to isolate one or two external dependencies.

The key trade-off is performance. @MockBean forces Spring to start (or reload) an application context, which takes seconds. @Mock needs no context at all and runs in milliseconds. As a rule: use @Mock for unit tests, use @MockBean for integration tests or slice tests where you need Spring's dependency injection to wire things together but want to stub specific beans.

A subtle but important point: @MockBean causes Spring's context caching to create a separate context for each unique combination of @MockBean declarations. If two test classes have different @MockBean sets, each gets its own context, slowing down the entire suite. This is why you should minimise @MockBean usage in integration tests.
